# Code signing for NOVA Windows installer (electron-builder)
#
# A paid Authenticode certificate (EV or OV from DigiCert, Sectigo, SSL.com, etc.)
# makes Windows SmartScreen trust NOVA-Setup.exe on first run.
#
# Vendors (examples — pick one and buy Code Signing):
#   https://www.digicert.com/signing/code-signing-certificates
#   https://sectigo.com/ssl-certificates-tls/code-signing
#   https://www.ssl.com/certificates/code-signing/
#
# Steps (local build):
# 1) Complete identity validation with the CA.
# 2) Export / receive a .pfx (or use a hardware token — set CSC_LINK accordingly).
# 3) In PowerShell before Open-NOVA-Packaged.bat / npm run electron:build:
#
#   set CSC_LINK=C:\path\to\certificate.pfx
#   set CSC_KEY_PASSWORD=your-cert-password
#
# Optional:
#   set CSC_IDENTITY_AUTO_DISCOVERY=false
#   set WIN_CSC_LINK=...   (same as CSC_LINK on Windows)
#
# GitHub Actions (tag release on DrMeji/Nova-Source):
#   Repo secrets:
#     CSC_LINK          — base64 of the .pfx (or path if staged on the runner)
#     CSC_KEY_PASSWORD  — pfx password
#   Workflow passes CSC_* into electron-builder when secrets exist.
#   Releases (installer + latest.yml + blockmap) publish to Nova-Source first;
#   public DrMeji/Nova is opt-in via publish_public.
#
# electron-builder picks these up automatically — never commit secrets.
#
# Verify after build (PowerShell):
#   Get-AuthenticodeSignature .\release\NOVA-Setup.exe
#   Status should be Valid when the cert is trusted.
#
# In the app: Settings → Updates → “Check this install’s signature”
# (only meaningful on a packaged install, not vite/dev).
#
# Reputation / SmartScreen:
# After you ship signed builds, Windows reputation improves as installs grow.
# EV certificates typically skip the long “unknown publisher” period faster than OV.
# Signing alone does not instantly remove SmartScreen for brand-new publishers.
#
# ── In-app updates / delta (latest.yml) ─────────────────────────────────────
# electron-builder writes release/latest.yml + NOVA-Setup.exe.blockmap when
# differentialPackage is true. The release workflow uploads both.
#
# After each tagged ship, verify:
#   1) https://github.com/DrMeji/Nova/releases/tag/vX.Y.Z has:
#        NOVA-Setup.exe, latest.yml, NOVA-Setup.exe.blockmap
#   2) Open an older packaged install → Settings → Updates → Check
#   3) Download should pull the new build (delta when blockmap matches)
#
# ── Plugin OAuth (optional, one-click device / browser login) ──────────────
# GitHub: https://github.com/settings/developers → New OAuth App → enable Device Flow
# Google: https://console.cloud.google.com/apis/credentials → OAuth client
#
#   set ARK_GITHUB_CLIENT_ID=your_oauth_app_client_id
#   set ARK_GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=...
#   set ARK_GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=...
#
# Or paste public client ids in Settings → Providers (secrets stay in env).
#
# ── Local brain API (desktop loopback) ─────────────────────────────────────
# External apps (Lindsay, agents, etc.) use NOVA as their brain — not Ollama.
# Docs: docs/ark-brain-api.md · docs/lindsay-migration.md
# Desktop listens on http://127.0.0.1:8787 (Ollama-shaped /api/* + native /v1/*)
#   set NOVA_BRAIN_TOKEN=optional_bearer_token
